        <p>
          Establishing a scalable cosmetics or personal hygiene manufacturing facility demands high standards of quality assurance and standardized testing protocols. Small mistakes in water quality or batch order of addition can lead to physical defects like cloudiness or structural separation during storage. The following answers address standard technical inquiries from startups, helping you optimize your blending processes and guarantee compliance.
        </p>

        <div style="display: flex; flex-direction: column; gap: 20px; margin-top: 20px;">
          
          <div class="box" style="margin: 0; background: #ffffff; border: 1px solid var(--border); padding: 20px; border-radius: var(--radius-md);">
            <h3 style="color: var(--primary); font-size: 1.15rem; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 8px;">1. What is the standard pH for a liquid hand wash?</h3>
            <p style="margin: 0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 0.95rem;">
              The ideal pH for a hand wash is between 5.0 and 6.0. This range matches the naturally acidic pH of the skin, minimizing irritation.
            </p>
          </div>

          <div class="box" style="margin: 0; background: #ffffff; border: 1px solid var(--border); padding: 20px; border-radius: var(--radius-md);">
            <h3 style="color: var(--primary); font-size: 1.15rem; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 8px;">2. Why does my liquid soap turn cloudy in winter?</h3>
            <p style="margin: 0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 0.95rem;">
              Cloudiness is usually caused by the surfactants reaching their "Krafft point" or the thickeners losing solubility at low temperatures.
            </p>
